@only-if workflows
# __NAME__ infrastructure

Two Terraform roots: `foundation` (slow, shared resources) and `instance` (per-deployment resources). See
`azure:structure` for why they're split. Both are driven by the app profile at `infra/app.json`, written by
the app stack; `azure:scaffold` turns it into the workload, host and database resources it asks for. Run
`python -B <scaffold-skill-dir>/scripts/scaffold.py options --schema` to see azure's own, app-agnostic
options as JSON.

## Bootstrap

Foundation starts with local state, using a personal Azure account kept isolated from any other CLI session:

```sh
export AZURE_CONFIG_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
az login
cd infra/foundation
terraform init
terraform apply -var-file=../env/ci.tfvars
```

Then migrate into the state storage account `apply` just created:

```sh
cat > backend.auto.tfvars.json <<JSON
{}
JSON
terraform init -migrate-state \
  -backend-config="resource_group_name=$(terraform output -raw resource_group_name)" \
  -backend-config="storage_account_name=$(terraform output -raw state_storage_account_name)" \
  -backend-config="container_name=tfstate" \
  -backend-config="key=__NAME__/foundation.tfstate"
```

`instance` uses the same account with key `__NAME__/instance.tfstate`, or
`__NAME__/instance.<ephemeral_id>.tfstate` for a PR's preview instance.

`infra/.gitignore` excludes `.terraform/`, state, plan and crash files and any `override.tf*`; it does not
ignore `.terraform.lock.hcl`, which `azure:build` requires to be committed.

## GitHub environments and repository variables

Create three GitHub environments: `ci` (the shared non-production environment `ci.yml` deploys), `preview`
(a pull request's ephemeral instance) and `prod`. Give `preview` and `prod` protection rules in repository
settings — at minimum, restrict `prod` to deployments from `v*` tags; a preview or production deploy should
not run unreviewed.

`pr.yml`, `ci.yml`, `prod.yml`, `pr-closed.yml`, `deploy-environment.yml` and `destroy-preview.yml` read
these GitHub Actions variables, set once the foundation has deployed:

- `AZURE_FOUNDATION_READY` — `"true"` once the state account above exists; gates `plan`, `deploy-ci` and
  `deploy-preview`.
- `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` — the deploy identity's client id (`terraform output -raw deploy_identity_client_id`).
- `AZURE_TENANT_ID`, `AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` — the target tenant and subscription.
- `TFSTATE_RESOURCE_GROUP`, `TFSTATE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT` — the values used in the migration above.

Set these on the `ci` environment, and again on `prod` with that environment's own subscription and storage
account. A PR's preview instance deploys and destroys through the `preview` environment's values, which
should match `ci`'s, since a preview reuses `ci`'s foundation — only its `instance` root is ephemeral. The
`ci` foundation's deploy identity carries a federated credential for both the `environment:ci` and
`environment:preview` OIDC subjects, so the same identity can sign in under either GitHub environment.

A pull request's preview is opt-in: add the `deploy-preview` label to the PR to deploy it, and `pr-closed.yml`
destroys it on close only if it carried that label. Applying a label requires the repository's `triage` role,
which is what makes the label a safe trust gate — a pull request from a fork can't grant itself a preview
deploy by labelling its own PR. `plan`, `deploy-preview`, the state-lock release and `destroy-preview` also
check `github.event.pull_request.head.repo.full_name == github.repository` as defense in depth.

Update `infra/env/ci.tfvars` (and `infra/env/prod.tfvars`) with the real GitHub owner, repository and
their numeric ids:

```sh
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo> --jq '.owner.id, .id'
```

The first line is `github_owner_id`, the second is `github_repository_id`.

The federated credentials expect GitHub's immutable OIDC subject, `repo:<owner>@<owner-id>/<repo>@<repo-id>:...`,
which repositories created after 2026-07-15 send by default. Opt an older repository into immutable subjects
in its Actions OIDC settings before the first plan, or no login will match.

`database.schemas` (default `["public"]`) lists every Postgres schema a migration needs; the migration job
creates each non-`public` one and grants every workload identity the same privileges on it as on `public`.

## The deploy workflow

`deploy-environment.yml` is a reusable workflow (`workflow_call`) that `ci.yml` (environment `ci`),
`prod.yml` (environment `prod`, `prod.tfvars`) and `pr.yml`'s preview job (environment `preview`, ephemeral)
all call. It applies foundation unless deploying a preview — a preview reuses `ci`'s foundation — then for
every workload in the profile runs its `build.image` or `build.package` command — whichever its host
needs — through the `build-workload` composite action, with `REGISTRY` (the registry login server),
`REPOSITORY` (`<name>-<id>`), `TAG` (the commit sha) and, for an image build,
`IMAGE=$REGISTRY/$REPOSITORY:$TAG` set in its environment. The database's `migration.build.image`, if any,
builds the same way under key `migration`. It then applies the migration job and its identity, admin and
database, runs the job and waits for it to succeed, applies the full instance plan with the built images,
deploys each package (Flex Consumption via `az functionapp deploy`; App Service via a staging-slot deploy,
a readiness wait, then a slot swap), and waits for each Container App's revision to become ready. If any
workload consumes a topic, it also removes each subscription's `$Default` rule, reading the subscription
list from a Terraform output rather than inlining it.

A pull request's preview instance deploys with the full `ephemeral_id=pr<N>` — never truncated, so two PRs
can never collide on the same preview's state key — through `destroy-preview.yml` using the same id on
close. The Terraform variable allows up to 6 lowercase alphanumeric characters; a PR number that doesn't fit
makes the id-computing step fail loudly (`::error::`, naming the limit) rather than deploy under a shortened,
possibly colliding id. The preview apply and its eventual teardown share one `preview-<id>` concurrency
group, and a `break-preview-lock` job breaks the state blob's lease over OIDC if a new push cancels an
in-flight preview apply.

If the profile has a top-level `verify` list, `pr.yml` and `ci.yml` run each command in a sentence-named
`verify` job before any Azure job.

## Offline gate

Run this for each root before pushing, and in CI without any Azure login:

```sh
terraform fmt -check -recursive
terraform init -backend=false
terraform validate
```
